---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="DMARC Alignment: Relaxed vs Strict Explained"
  description="DMARC alignment explained: how the From domain must match SPF or DKIM authenticated identities, relaxed vs strict modes, and why mail can pass SPF but fail DMARC."
 breadcrumb={[["Home", "/"], ["Email Authentication", "/email-authentication/"], ["DMARC Alignment"]]}>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a> <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/email-authentication/" class="hover:text-accent">Email Authentication</a> <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">DMARC Alignment</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">DMARC Alignment: Relaxed vs Strict Explained</h1>
    <div class="pd-answer" data-vs="answer">
      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
      DMARC alignment is the rule that connects authentication results to the domain the recipient actually sees. A message can pass SPF and pass DKIM and still fail DMARC - because the authenticated domains do not match the visible From domain. This page explains why that happens and what to change.
    </p>
    </div>
<nav class="pd-toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <p class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-accent">On this page</p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="#the-three-domains-involved">The three domains involved</a></li>
        <li><a href="#how-dmarc-uses-these-domains">How DMARC uses these domains</a></li>
        <li><a href="#relaxed-vs-strict-alignment">Relaxed vs strict alignment</a></li>
        <li><a href="#synthetic-examples-labeled-not-customer-">Synthetic examples (labeled, not customer evidence)</a></li>
        <li><a href="#fixing-alignment-failures">Fixing alignment failures</a></li>
        <li><a href="#what-our-dns-based-tools-can-and-cannot-">What our DNS-based tools can and cannot determine</a></li>
        <li><a href="#related">Related</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>

    <div class="rich-text mt-8">
      <h2 id="the-three-domains-involved">The three domains involved</h2>
      <p>Every email carries three domain identities that DMARC compares:</p>
      <div class="mt-3 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60">
        <table class="pdns-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Identity</th><th>Where it appears</th><th>What it controls</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td class="text-sm font-medium text-ink">From domain</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">The visible <code class="font-mono">From:</code> header</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">What the recipient sees; the domain DMARC protects</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="text-sm font-medium text-ink">SPF domain</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">The <code class="font-mono">Return-Path</code> (envelope from)</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Which server is authorized to send</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="text-sm font-medium text-ink">DKIM domain</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">The <code class="font-mono">d=</code> tag in the DKIM-Signature</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Who cryptographically signed the message</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2 id="how-dmarc-uses-these-domains">How DMARC uses these domains</h2>
      <p>
        DMARC checks whether the SPF-authenticated domain or the DKIM-authenticated domain <strong class="text-ink">aligns</strong> with the From domain. Only one needs to align - DMARC passes if <em>either</em> aligned SPF pass <em>or</em> aligned DKIM pass exists. It does not require both. This is defined in RFC 7489 <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7489#section-3.1" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft" rel="noopener">§3.1</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="relaxed-vs-strict-alignment">Relaxed vs strict alignment</h2>
      <p>
        The alignment mode is controlled by the <code class="font-mono">aspf</code> (for SPF) and <code class="font-mono">adkim</code> (for DKIM) tags in your DMARC record. Both default to <strong class="text-ink">relaxed</strong>.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-3 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60">
        <table class="pdns-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>SPF alignment rule</th><th>DKIM alignment rule</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td class="text-sm font-medium text-ink">Relaxed (default)</td>
              <td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">The SPF domain and From domain must share the same organizational domain (e.g., <code class="font-mono">mail.example.com</code> aligns with <code class="font-mono">example.com</code>)</td>
              <td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">The DKIM <code class="font-mono">d=</code> domain and From domain must share the same organizational domain</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="text-sm font-medium text-ink">Strict</td>
              <td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">The SPF domain must exactly match the From domain</td>
              <td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">The DKIM <code class="font-mono">d=</code> domain must exactly match the From domain</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2 id="synthetic-examples-labeled-not-customer-">Synthetic examples (labeled, not customer evidence)</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-ink-faint">All examples use reserved documentation domains (<code class="font-mono">example.com</code>, <code class="font-mono">example.net</code>, <code class="font-mono">example.org</code>) per RFC 2606. These are synthetic illustrations of the alignment rules, not output from any tool.</p>

      <h3>Example 1: SPF and DKIM pass, but DMARC fails (alignment failure)</h3>
      <pre class="mt-2 rounded-lg border border-line bg-panel-2 p-4 font-mono text-xs leading-relaxed text-accent-soft">From: marketing@example.com
Return-Path: bounce@esp.example.net   ← SPF evaluated against esp.example.net
DKIM-Signature: d=esp.example.net    ← DKIM signed by esp.example.net

SPF result:  pass  (sender IP authorized by esp.example.net's SPF)
DKIM result: pass  (signature verifies against esp.example.net's key)
DMARC result: fail  (neither esp.example.net aligns with example.com)</pre>
      <p class="mt-2 text-xs text-ink-faint">Synthetic example. The ESP authenticates using its own domain for both Return-Path and DKIM. Authentication passed, but neither authenticated identity aligns with the visible From domain.</p>

      <h3>Example 2: SPF fails, but DMARC passes via aligned DKIM</h3>
      <pre class="mt-2 rounded-lg border border-line bg-panel-2 p-4 font-mono text-xs leading-relaxed text-accent-soft">From: newsletter@example.com
Return-Path: bounce@other.example.net  ← SPF fails (IP not authorized)
DKIM-Signature: d=example.com         ← DKIM signed by example.com

SPF result:  fail  (IP not authorized by other.example.net's SPF)
DKIM result: pass  (signature verifies; d=example.com aligns with From: example.com)
DMARC result: pass  (one aligned DKIM pass is sufficient)</pre>
      <p class="mt-2 text-xs text-ink-faint">Synthetic example. DMARC passes because one aligned authentication (DKIM) succeeded. SPF failure alone does not cause DMARC failure when DKIM is aligned - DMARC requires only one passing, aligned mechanism.</p>

      <h3>Example 3: Forwarded mail with rewritten envelope</h3>
      <p class="text-sm text-ink-dim">
        In this scenario, a forwarder receives a message from <code class="font-mono">user@example.com</code> and re-sends it. The forwarder rewrites the envelope (Return-Path) to its own bounce address, but does not modify the message body or headers.
      </p>
      <pre class="mt-2 rounded-lg border border-line bg-panel-2 p-4 font-mono text-xs leading-relaxed text-accent-soft">From: user@example.com
Return-Path: bounce@forwarder.example.org  ← Forwarder rewrote the envelope
DKIM-Signature: d=example.com             ← Original signature intact

SPF check (at final recipient):
  Connecting IP: forwarder.example.org's server
  Return-Path domain: forwarder.example.org
  → SPF is evaluated against forwarder.example.org's own policy
  → Result depends on whether the forwarder's IP is authorized
    by forwarder.example.org's SPF record (not example.com's)

DKIM check:
  Signature d=example.com, body unmodified
  → DKIM pass (if the signature survives; modifications by
    the forwarder can invalidate it)

DMARC check:
  From domain: example.com
  Aligned SPF? SPF was checked against forwarder.example.org,
    which does not align with example.com → No aligned SPF pass.
  Aligned DKIM? d=example.com aligns with From: example.com → Yes.
  → DMARC pass (one aligned DKIM pass is sufficient)</pre>
      <p class="mt-2 text-xs text-ink-faint">Synthetic example. The forwarder rewrote the envelope, so SPF is evaluated against the forwarder's domain - not the original sender's. DKIM may survive if the forwarder does not modify the signed content; modifications (adding footers, re-encoding) can invalidate the signature.</p>

      <h3>Example 4: Forwarded mail with preserved envelope</h3>
      <p class="text-sm text-ink-dim">
        Some forwarders preserve the original envelope. In this case, SPF is evaluated against the original sender's domain - but the connecting IP belongs to the forwarder, which is typically not authorized by the original sender's SPF record.
      </p>
      <pre class="mt-2 rounded-lg border border-line bg-panel-2 p-4 font-mono text-xs leading-relaxed text-accent-soft">From: user@example.com
Return-Path: user@example.com          ← Original envelope preserved
DKIM-Signature: d=example.com         ← Original signature intact

SPF check (at final recipient):
  Connecting IP: forwarder.example.org's server
  Return-Path domain: example.com
  → SPF evaluated against example.com's policy
  → Forwarder's IP is typically not in example.com's SPF
  → SPF result: fail

DKIM check:
  Signature d=example.com, body unmodified
  → DKIM pass

DMARC check:
  Aligned SPF? example.com's SPF failed (forwarder IP not authorized) → No
  Aligned DKIM? d=example.com aligns with From: example.com → Yes
  → DMARC pass</pre>
      <p class="mt-2 text-xs text-ink-faint">Synthetic example. In both forwarding scenarios (rewritten or preserved envelope), SPF typically fails at the final recipient. DKIM can provide the aligned pass - but only if the signature survives any modifications made during forwarding.</p>

      <h2 id="fixing-alignment-failures">Fixing alignment failures</h2>
      <p>
        The fix depends on your sending setup and your email provider's capabilities. There is no single universal DNS change.
      </p>
      <ol class="list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-5">
        <li><strong class="text-ink">Identify which sender is failing.</strong> Check your DMARC aggregate reports (or send test messages and read the <code class="font-mono">Authentication-Results</code> header) to see which service's mail is failing alignment.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-ink">Configure the ESP to use your domain.</strong> Many ESPs support custom Return-Path (bounce domain) and custom DKIM signing. Availability varies by provider and plan - check your provider's documentation to confirm what they offer and how to enable it. If supported, configure both so alignment passes. See our <a href="/email-authentication/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">ESP setup directory</a> for provider-specific guides.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-ink">Verify with a test message.</strong> Send a test from the ESP and inspect the <code class="font-mono">Authentication-Results</code> header. At least one of <code class="font-mono">spf=pass</code> or <code class="font-mono">dkim=pass</code> should appear with your domain - either aligned mechanism is sufficient for DMARC pass.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-ink">Check alignment mode.</strong> If you use subdomains for sending, relaxed alignment (the default) is usually sufficient. Strict alignment requires exact domain matching and may not be necessary for your use case.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 id="what-our-dns-based-tools-can-and-cannot-">What our DNS-based tools can and cannot determine</h2>
      <p>
        The <a href="/dmarc-record-checker/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">DMARC Record Checker</a> inspects your published DMARC record (policy, reporting, alignment mode tags). It cannot evaluate a specific message's alignment - that requires reading the <code class="font-mono">Authentication-Results</code> header on a received message. DNS-based tools verify that the policy is correctly published; message-level inspection verifies that the policy is being satisfied by your senders.
      </p>

